Sending keys: ["cat > /app/output/answer.txt << 'EOF'\nMedical Case Analysis: 45-year-old Male with Psoriasis and Joint Symptoms\n\nMost Likely Diagnosis: Psoriatic Arthritis\n\nTwo Recommended Diagnostic Tests:\n\n1. Rheumatoid Factor (RF) and Anti-Cyclic Citrullinated Peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies\n2. X-rays of affected joints (knees and any other symptomatic joints)\n\nReasoning:\nThe patient presents with established psoriasis and is receiving knee injections for joint symptoms, combined with the characteristic distribution of skin lesions including palmoplantar involvement (palms and soles). This clinical presentation strongly suggests psoriatic arthritis. \n\n- RF and anti-CCP testing helps differentiate psoriatic arthritis (typically seronegative) from rheumatoid arthritis (often seropositive)\n- Joint imaging (X-rays) can reveal characteristic changes of psoriatic arthritis such as joint space narrowing, erosions, and new bone formation\n\nPatient Counseling on Next Steps:\n\n1. Explain that psoriatic arthritis is a chronic inflammatory condition affecting both skin and joints\n2. Emphasize the importance of early treatment to prevent joint damage and disability\n3. Discuss referral to a rheumatologist for specialized management\n4. Review treatment options which may include:\n   - Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) like methotrexate\n   - Biologic therapies that can treat both skin and joint symptoms\n   - Continued orthopedic care for joint management as needed\n5. Stress the importance of regular monitoring and follow-up\n6. Discuss lifestyle modifications including exercise, weight management, and joint protection strategies\n7. Address any concerns about the chronic nature of the condition while emphasizing that effective treatments are available\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.1 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
